# Merge drivers for the ledgers that slices append to (slice BL1b, 20 November 2026).
#
# ADR_RESERVATIONS.md takes git's union merge: two branches that each add a reservation row both
# keep it, instead of bouncing the merge. This is safe for this file alone, because its changes are
# added rows (in the Reservations table, above "## Baseline") and one in-place edit, a status flip of
# a row from "reserved" to "written". What union does with that flip was measured with a real
# `git merge` (tests/test_ledger_union_merge.py, the flip-and-append case): when one branch flips a
# row and the other adds a row on the very next line (the usual shape: the orchestrator reserves a
# row, the slice flips it, another slice appends after it), git does not combine the two changes and
# union keeps both sides, so the merge is clean but leaves the flipped row TWICE (once reserved,
# once written) beside the new row, in an order that depends on which branch is merged first. It is
# not silently wrong: the duplicate check (tools/adr_reservations_check.py, "reserved 2 times", in
# make lanes-check) reports it and so does the --against comparison, so the merge is bounced by the
# checks, not by git. Any other edit to a row (a title, number, lane or date, a removed row) is
# caught by the --against mode, which compares with the merge-base and allows only rows added above
# "## Baseline" and that one flip (tests/test_adr_reservations.py).
#
# spec/06_DECISIONS.md is NOT listed. Its amendments are appended blocks, but it also has content edited
# in place (the status register near the top, and amendment bodies edited more than once), and when two
# branches edit the same line union keeps both versions, so the line would be doubled. Adding it needs
# the duplicate-heading and doubled-line checks on main first, and the owner's word.
#
# The attributes are read from the tree being merged, so this applies to every merge into main once the
# file is on main; a branch that brings the file in does not get it for its own merge of main.
OspreyFlow_Build/spec/ADR_RESERVATIONS.md merge=union
#
# The GOV.UK tree's two page lists take union too (the owner's word of 27 November 2026: page parts kept conflicting in them,
# which broke trains and cost rounds). A page adds a line to each and edits none:
# - apps/pages/tree-govuk/routes.yaml: its path, at the end, one to a line;
# - apps/pages/server/pageList.ts: its import, one to a line, and its module, one to a line in `PAGES`.
# Neither holds a count of the pages or a sentence naming each, since two branches editing the same line keep both
# versions under union. tests/glass_trees/test_page_lists_merge.py merges two pages side by side with git (both kept, in
# either order) and checks the real lists at every gate for what union could leave: a path twice, a line broken, a page
# imported or listed twice, one listed and not imported. test/govuk/help.test.ts (HP9) holds the two lists in step, and
# the type check holds pageList.ts to TypeScript.
#
# Not listed, though each page touches them:
# - spec/08_ACCEPTANCE_SCENARIOS.md: a page's scenario is a new section, but two parts of the same page edit the same
#   paragraphs in place (GR1b and GR1e both edit Scenario CS), and union would keep both versions of a paragraph, silently.
# - apps/pages/scripts/build.mjs: it has no list now; it bundles the server files that import the shared package,
#   found by reading them (scripts/sharedEntries.mjs).
# - apps/pages/FUNCTIONS.yaml: a page fills in its own row's fields, an edit in place.
# - the tests (test/govuk/help.test.ts and the scenario tests): no test pins the whole route list or counts the pages
#   any more (tests/glass_trees/test_route_pins.py), so a page edits none but its own.
OspreyFlow_Core/apps/pages/tree-govuk/routes.yaml merge=union
OspreyFlow_Core/apps/pages/server/pageList.ts merge=union
